<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Psalm 104</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as תהילים קד, Tehillim 104</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Psalm 104 is the 104th psalm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Book of Psalms</a>; in the slightly different numbering of the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Vulgate it is Psalm 103, known in Latin as &quot;Benedic anima mea Domino&quot;. One of the longer psalms, it is traditionally divided into 35 verses.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hymns to the gods were inscribed in temples and tombs; the Great Hymn to the Aten, from the time of Akhenaten, celebrates the sun as the creator of all life in terms often compared with <b>Psalm 104</b> of the Hebrew Bible.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>James Henry Breasted noted its likeness to <b>Psalm 104</b> and believed the hymn inspired the psalm; Arthur Weigall set the two texts side by side and judged a direct connection likely, either through a common Syrian source or by descent from the Egyptian poem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Psalm 104</b> is the 104th psalm of the Book of Psalms; in the slightly different numbering of the Greek Septuagint and the Latin Vulgate it is Psalm 103, known in Latin as &quot;Benedic anima mea Domino&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-104">Psalm 104</a></li></ul></section>
